<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mon people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mon are a people of Lower Myanmar and central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> who speak a Mon-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> (Austroasiatic) language. They were among the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist peoples of mainland South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, associated with the Dvaravati culture and with kingdoms such as Thaton, Hariphunchai and Hanthawaddy. Their script and religious learning shaped Burmese and northern Thai culture, and many Mon fled to Siam after Burmese conquests in the 18th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mon-people.jpg" alt="Mon people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninjastrikers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mon_State_(2018).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1740 the <b>Mon</b> of Lower Burma rebelled, and the forces of the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom, after a decade of fighting, advanced up the Irrawaddy and took Ava in 1752.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The war ended the <b>Mon people</b>'s centuries-long dominance of the south.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of the Pagan Kingdom, Wareru, a <b>Mon</b> adventurer said to have been a merchant, seized Martaban in 1287 and gained recognition from Sukhothai.</blockquote><a href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>She brought <b>Mon</b> Buddhist monks and craftsmen from the Dvaravati world, and one of her twin sons founded Lampang as a second centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/hariphunchai">Hariphunchai</a></li><li><blockquote>Lavo emerged as one of the <b>Mon</b> towns of the Dvaravati world, with Lopburi as its centre from about the 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/lavo-kingdom">Lavo Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>He built a fort and church, encouraged Catholic conversions and allied with local <b>Mon</b> leaders.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-syriam">Portuguese Syriam</a></li></ul></section>
